    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "those are good starting points, but they feel a little bit generic, you know? like, they could be in any city. since we're going for that \"gritty realism\" and trying to represent a real urban environment, how would you flesh these out in terms of their backgrounds? like, when you're thinking about the specific demographics for the detectives versus the people they're chasing, how do you make those casting choices feel authentic to the neighborhood?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To make it feel authentic, you should lean into the established dynamics of the neighborhood. The detectives could be from the same background as the community they patrol, giving them a conflicted, insider perspective. Conversely, the criminals should reflect the specific socioeconomic struggles of that area, perhaps drawing from groups that are historically marginalized. This contrast in background will naturally fuel the drama and the tension in your script.",
      "source": "target",
